00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:23.700
And then, seeing that
as a total net value.

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:27.700
Now, that's true-- reducing your
total cost is a great thing.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:30.360
But the fact of the matter
is, it's not an adequate way

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:33.590
to think about the big picture.

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:36.500
The truth about cloud is
that lifting and shifting

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:39.860
your workloads is not
really the entire picture,

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:42.980
and does not transform
your IT by itself.

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:46.830
Instead, you should really
think about bridging the gaps

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:51.200
in between your current business
processes and the modernization

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:55.310
and the innovation you can
achieve by moving to cloud.

00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:59.990
The more and more you use GCPs
technologies, the more and more

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:02.750
you're able to accelerate
the amount of value you

00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:05.880
capture from moving to cloud.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:09.740
So I like to think about
this is an iceberg picture.

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:12.470
In this instance, there's
the obvious costs,

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:15.740
which are using your compute,
using your other resources

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:16.870
on the cloud.

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:19.010
But then there's the
less obvious costs,

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:21.860
like, all that takes to
maintain that infrastructure

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:23.270
on-premises.

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:27.170
And then there's the value that
you get from abstracting away

00:05:27.170 --> 00:05:30.050
your concerns about
all the cloud work

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:33.260
you do on-premises to the cloud.

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:36.080
And then there's the less
obvious value you get

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:40.260
such as efficiencies,
productivity, reliability,

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:41.580
security.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:44.060
So let's alter this
equation just a little bit,

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:48.020
to make sure that we get the
full value of moving to cloud.

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:50.300
Really, the change
in net value that you

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:53.780
can enjoy when you move to
cloud is really the total value

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:55.790
of being in the cloud itself--

00:05:55.790 --> 00:05:57.390
all the things I just mentioned.

00:05:57.390 --> 00:05:59.732
And then you subtract from
that, the total costs.

00:05:59.732 --> 00:06:01.190
Now for all of you
guys are saying,

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:04.740
Debo, it's way too early for me
to be doing math this morning--

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:05.900
let's simplify it.

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:08.420
Just think about
your total value.

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:11.570
And just think about
your total costs.

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:15.260
This has worked
fantastically for our friends

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:16.850
over at Vendasta.

00:06:16.850 --> 00:06:20.030
They focus on doing just
that-- getting their value out

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:21.950
of a lot of GCP products.

00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:25.730
And then analyzing that value
using BigQuery and a number

00:06:25.730 --> 00:06:27.620
of cost management tools.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:31.010
They've been able to grow
their business by 32%,

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:33.590
while keeping their
costs relatively flat.

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:35.840
I imagine a lot of you
could do the same thing

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:38.720
by using Google Cloud.

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:41.240
So let's stop talking about
this in an abstract way,

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:43.940
and let's talk about it a
little bit more concrete way.

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:47.480
Let's focus on GCE, or
Google Compute Engine,

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:49.580
as one of the first
products we can show you

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:53.370
how to drive more value.

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:55.980
The typical costs
of compute ownership

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:59.530
are spread across a
number of elements.

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:00.940
One of them is the building.

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:02.280
You've got to pay the rent.

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:04.980
You've got to cover the
electricity, the lighting,

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:07.960
the AC, and the security.

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:11.250
You've got to think about the
software, operating systems,

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:15.000
management utilities,
software licenses.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:16.950
You've also got to
think about people--

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:20.880
all the specialists, the
consultants, your employees,

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:22.470
and HR.

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:24.780
Oh, yeah, there's one
more thing we forgot--

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:26.100
the hardware.

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:28.830
You've got to make sure that
your compute is taken care

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:31.080
of on-premises so
you can actually run

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:33.330
the workloads you care about.

00:07:33.330 --> 00:07:37.060
So there's not just that you
have to think about, as well.

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:39.750
You also got to think about
how you actually drive

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:41.620
the value of your business.

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:43.800
Let's start with
the hardware costs.

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:46.980
Every 18 months or so, your
hardware, more or less,

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:48.330
becomes out-of-date.

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:51.443
And to keep that up-to-date
has a cost associated with it,

00:07:51.443 --> 00:07:53.610
if you want to make sure
that you remain competitive

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:55.680
with those in your industry.

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:59.010
You also want to take advantage
of all kinds of innovations

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:02.460
that are fairly expensive to
come up with your own on-prem,

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:07.440
such as, GPUs TPUs, artificial
intelligence, and machine

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:08.280
learning.

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:11.100
You also want to think
about how you might serve

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:12.600
a new region of the world--

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:14.820
let's say-- if you want
to globally expand.

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:17.940
Or let's say, that your
business is doing fantastic,

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:20.980
and you want to make sure
you can scale very quickly,

00:08:20.980 --> 00:08:23.470
especially if it's not expected.

00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:27.230
And so that's where we
enter Google Compute Engine.

00:08:27.230 --> 00:08:31.140
The simplicity and elegance
of it is its core value.

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:33.000
You start the virtual
machines that you

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:35.789
need for your workloads
when you need them.

00:08:35.789 --> 00:08:39.100
You resize them and
scale, as necessary.

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:40.860
And then you shut
down that VM when

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:42.960
you're not using it anymore.

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:47.700
The key to that is you pay
only for what you need.

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:49.860
Well, you guys didn't come
for the base explanation

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:51.777
of what a virtual machine
is or how to use it.

00:08:51.777 --> 00:08:53.700
So you really came
for finding out

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:56.910
how you can drive even
more value out of using

00:08:56.910 --> 00:08:58.862
GCE and other products.

00:08:58.862 --> 00:08:59.820
And that's very simple.

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:04.770
I like to spread it into two
elements-- behaviors and tools.

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:06.720
On the behavior
side, you definitely

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:09.480
want to analyze and
plan your workloads.

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:11.070
Think about seasonality.

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:12.452
Think about the duration.

00:09:12.452 --> 00:09:14.160
Think about the value
of your workloads--

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:15.600
are they mission critical?

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:17.940
Figure out how you want
to segment those, and use

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:20.050
the right machines for them.

00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:21.510
The second thing
I tell you to do

00:09:21.510 --> 00:09:24.330
is think about virtual
machine types or families.

00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:26.280
Now, we offer a number
of different ones.

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:28.830
Of course, there's
general purpose VMs,

00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:32.340
but there's also different
optimized VM families that you

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:33.630
should take advantage of.

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:36.450
For example,
compute-optimized VMs,

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:38.430
if you want to think
about performance.

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:40.830
Or memory-optimized
VMs, if your storage

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:42.120
is very important to you.

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:44.910
Or platform-optimized
VMs, if you're

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:48.300
thinking about specific types of
operating systems or platforms

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:50.520
that you want your virtual
machines to run on.

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:52.200
Then, of course,
the third behavior

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:57.420
you really want to focus on is
resize the VMs as necessary.

00:09:57.420 --> 00:10:01.410
Think about VMs being built
of core and RAM and sometimes,

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:04.500
additional attributes--
like attach persistent disk

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:05.970
or other things like that.

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:09.060
Make sure that you're using the
right size for what you need.

00:10:09.060 --> 00:10:12.030
When we move over to the
tools part of what you're

00:10:12.030 --> 00:10:14.340
going to do with
your VMs, you want

00:10:14.340 --> 00:10:17.420
to make sure to take advantage
of the Stackdriver API.

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:20.490
Now, the Stackdriver
API is fantastic

00:10:20.490 --> 00:10:24.580
because it's really giving
you logging, monitoring,

00:10:24.580 --> 00:10:28.200
and alerting right at your
fingertips, programmatically.

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:30.330
On top of that, we
give you what we

00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:33.110
like to call right-sizing
recommendations.

00:10:33.110 --> 00:10:34.950
Right-sizing
recommendations allow

00:10:34.950 --> 00:10:37.710
you to have the
proper size of VM

00:10:37.710 --> 00:10:40.140
after we've watched you
use those VMs for a given

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:43.440
period of time, in order
for you to save money or be

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:45.880
efficient in the
way you use them.

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:47.790
And finally, there's a
number of other tools

00:10:47.790 --> 00:10:52.140
that we'd like for you to use,
such as VPCs or sole tenancy--

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:56.160
which means just having your
workloads alone on a VM.

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:59.310
Access control,
snapshotting of disk images

00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:00.870
to make sure that
you can get quickly

00:11:00.870 --> 00:11:04.895
running with your VM, and
other types of tools like that.

00:11:04.895 --> 00:11:07.270
Now I told you in that equation
earlier that I mentioned,

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:08.560
it's not just about value.

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:10.630
It's about decreasing
cost, as well.

00:11:10.630 --> 00:11:13.820
So let's take a look at a few
of the ways you can do that.

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:15.373
We have custom machine types.

00:11:15.373 --> 00:11:17.290
One of the things that's
interesting about all

00:11:17.290 --> 00:11:19.690
those pre-determined machine
types that I just mentioned

00:11:19.690 --> 00:11:22.000
in the previous
slide is that they

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:25.510
tend to follow a specific
ratio of core to RAM.

00:11:25.510 --> 00:11:27.870
If you need to, you
can break that ratio

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:29.530
and have a custom
machine type that's

00:11:29.530 --> 00:11:32.390
flexible and well-priced.

00:11:32.390 --> 00:11:35.350
If you want to have a workload
that can withstand some

00:11:35.350 --> 00:11:38.230
interruption-- let's say,
once every 24 hours--

00:11:38.230 --> 00:11:41.090
you might want to think
about using preemptable VMs.

00:11:41.090 --> 00:11:43.510
Preemptable VMs
might get preempted--

00:11:43.510 --> 00:11:45.340
as their name suggests--

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:47.920
and they're cheaper.

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:50.772
You can also use
sustained use discounts.

00:11:50.772 --> 00:11:52.980
Now, this is a great thing
that you get automatically

00:11:52.980 --> 00:11:54.702
for long-running workloads.

00:11:54.702 --> 00:11:56.160
When you're workload's
going to run

00:11:56.160 --> 00:11:58.080
on a specific type of family--

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:01.410
the longer and longer you
use it, the more effective

00:12:01.410 --> 00:12:03.270
discounting you get.

00:12:03.270 --> 00:12:05.940
And finally, there's
committed use discounts.

00:12:05.940 --> 00:12:08.700
When you commit to using
a certain amount of core

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:12.630
and RAM per month on a
one or three year basis,

00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:16.680
we're able to effectively scale
and plan capacity for you.

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:19.230
That planning equals
savings on our part,

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:24.320
and we're more than happy to
share the discount with you.

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:27.400
Now, I've talked a little bit
more specifically about GCE,

00:12:27.400 --> 00:12:29.980
but there's a number
of different benefits

00:12:29.980 --> 00:12:32.830
you can enjoy across
all GCP products.

00:12:32.830 --> 00:12:34.510
And that includes,
but is not limited

00:12:34.510 --> 00:12:38.597
to the fact, that we have
planetary scale and networking.

00:12:38.597 --> 00:12:40.930
These are not just the services
we're using to help you.

00:12:40.930 --> 00:12:42.760
But they're also the
same types of services

00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:45.160
we use to run many of
our most famous products,

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:48.610
like, YouTube,
Gmail, and G Suite.

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:52.730
We also have site
reliability and support.

00:12:52.730 --> 00:12:55.510
Think about them as very,
very intelligent babysitters

00:12:55.510 --> 00:12:56.483
for your workloads.

00:12:56.483 --> 00:12:58.150
Making sure that your
precious workloads

00:12:58.150 --> 00:13:01.480
don't do anything weird
while you need them to run.

00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:04.330
We're backed by Google
Security, which, of course,

00:13:04.330 --> 00:13:06.105
I don't need to tell
you how important it

00:13:06.105 --> 00:13:08.230
is to be secure when you're
running your workloads.

00:13:08.230 --> 00:13:12.700
And we've got GCP-wide
tooling, logging,

00:13:12.700 --> 00:13:16.550
and APIs to connect you to all
the resources that you need.

00:13:16.550 --> 00:13:20.800
So let's move forward
in the compute spectrum,

00:13:20.800 --> 00:13:22.262
where my partner,
Stephen, is going

00:13:22.262 --> 00:13:24.220
to talk to you a little
bit about all the value

00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:27.940
you can extract from products
in the compute spectrum,

00:13:27.940 --> 00:13:29.762
in terms of different
ways to manage them.

00:13:29.762 --> 00:13:31.470
STEPHEN ELLIOTT: All
right, thanks, Debo.

00:13:34.250 --> 00:13:37.460
So I'll spend a little time
talking about managed compute.

00:13:37.460 --> 00:13:41.510
And this is where we move
from being able to provision

00:13:41.510 --> 00:13:43.940
virtual machines to actually
managing the infrastructure

00:13:43.940 --> 00:13:45.773
and operations for you
so you don't have to.

00:13:45.773 --> 00:13:48.313
So just to get a sense of
where we are in the room,

00:13:48.313 --> 00:13:50.480
can I get a show of hands--
if you feel comfortable,

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:50.980
of course.

00:13:50.980 --> 00:13:52.730
You don't have to--
but if you think

00:13:52.730 --> 00:13:54.605
your teams and your
software application team

00:13:54.605 --> 00:13:57.980
spend at least 25% of the time
maintaining the operations

00:13:57.980 --> 00:13:59.960
of the infrastructure.

00:13:59.960 --> 00:14:01.650
Anyone thinks that's true?

00:14:01.650 --> 00:14:02.950
At least 25%?

00:14:02.950 --> 00:14:03.950
How about, at least 50%?

00:14:08.750 --> 00:14:11.730
So just in the
theme of this talk--

00:14:11.730 --> 00:14:13.790
which is to think about
both cost and value--

00:14:13.790 --> 00:14:16.040
just think about what would
happen if you could reduce

00:14:16.040 --> 00:14:17.570
the amount of time
the team spends

00:14:17.570 --> 00:14:19.700
on managing infrastructure,
managing operations,

00:14:19.700 --> 00:14:22.040
maintaining the applications,
doing version management.

00:14:22.040 --> 00:14:25.580
If you could reduce that by
half, or 3/4, or in some cases,

00:14:25.580 --> 00:14:29.060
just fully reduce it and really
just focus on writing the code.

00:14:29.060 --> 00:14:31.528
And there's a couple of sessions
in this talk where we'll

00:14:31.528 --> 00:14:32.820
be talk about managed services.

00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:34.820
And that's really the
game we're trying to play,

00:14:34.820 --> 00:14:37.930
which is help you focus on the
application you're building.

00:14:37.930 --> 00:14:40.698
So Debo talked about
Compute Engine--

00:14:40.698 --> 00:14:42.740
and that's on the left
side of the screen-- which

00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:45.538
is something that gives you
full control over the servers

00:14:45.538 --> 00:14:46.580
that you're provisioning.

00:14:46.580 --> 00:14:49.070
And then at the other
end of the spectrum

00:14:49.070 --> 00:14:52.040
is Cloud Functions, where
you just write the code,

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:54.500
and that function runs in
the cloud automatically

00:14:54.500 --> 00:14:57.050
provisioned, automatically
scaling with events

00:14:57.050 --> 00:14:57.950
that trigger them.

00:14:57.950 --> 00:14:59.533
And then in between,
we still give you

00:14:59.533 --> 00:15:03.330
a choice for how much you
want Google to manage for you.

00:15:03.330 --> 00:15:05.645
So let's talk a little
bit about those.

00:15:05.645 --> 00:15:06.890
What really is the choice?

00:15:06.890 --> 00:15:08.090
And I think, it's
a choice of what

00:15:08.090 --> 00:15:09.340
you feel comfortable managing.

00:15:09.340 --> 00:15:11.390
So in some cases,
people love scheduling.

00:15:11.390 --> 00:15:12.800
And people love looking at logs.

00:15:12.800 --> 00:15:15.230
And doing the monitoring
and having full control.

00:15:15.230 --> 00:15:19.730
But they're either applications
that don't need that level

00:15:19.730 --> 00:15:22.513
of control or the team
just doesn't-- that's not

00:15:22.513 --> 00:15:23.180
their expertise.

00:15:23.180 --> 00:15:24.513
That's not what they want to do.

00:15:24.513 --> 00:15:26.330
So the more and more
we can manage for you,

00:15:26.330 --> 00:15:28.372
the more we believe you
can focus on doing what's

00:15:28.372 --> 00:15:31.870
really important in the cloud.

00:15:31.870 --> 00:15:32.940
So how do we do this?

00:15:32.940 --> 00:15:35.880
So there's one word that really
captures how we do this--

00:15:35.880 --> 00:15:36.870
it's plastics.

00:15:36.870 --> 00:15:40.830
We just do it with plastics
or-- sorry-- containers.

00:15:40.830 --> 00:15:42.960
Containers are--
the way to think

00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:46.575
about this is in the legacy
world in bare metal servers,

00:15:46.575 --> 00:15:47.700
you're managing everything.

00:15:47.700 --> 00:15:50.070
You're managing the hardware,
the racks, the operating

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:50.670
systems--

00:15:50.670 --> 00:15:51.480
you're updating those.

00:15:51.480 --> 00:15:52.855
The library's
making sure they're

00:15:52.855 --> 00:15:54.508
consistent across deployments.

00:15:54.508 --> 00:15:56.550
But what if we could manage
the hardware for you?

00:15:56.550 --> 00:15:57.050
Great.

00:15:57.050 --> 00:15:59.567
So now you are able
to rent servers.

00:15:59.567 --> 00:16:01.650
We can virtualize that to
make that more efficient

00:16:01.650 --> 00:16:04.110
and rent servers
through something, like,

00:16:04.110 --> 00:16:04.960
Compute Engine.

00:16:04.960 --> 00:16:07.210
But what if we could manage
that even further and say,

00:16:07.210 --> 00:16:08.590
well, don't worry about
the operating system.

00:16:08.590 --> 00:16:11.130
Don't worry about other security
updates or library updates.

00:16:11.130 --> 00:16:13.680
Just deliver that
hermetically-sealed piece

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:16.072
of code that contains all
the library dependencies,

00:16:16.072 --> 00:16:17.530
all the code that
you want to run--

00:16:17.530 --> 00:16:18.720
that's a container.

00:16:18.720 --> 00:16:20.500
And they don't interfere
with each other.

00:16:20.500 --> 00:16:22.163
And so what you
deploy is what runs.

00:16:22.163 --> 00:16:24.330
And then if we can manage
that even further and say,

00:16:24.330 --> 00:16:25.788
don't even think
about the library.

00:16:25.788 --> 00:16:28.140
Just write the Python,
write the code,

00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:30.960
write the JavaScript
that you want to run.

00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:33.610
And if it's a common
version or flavor of it,

00:16:33.610 --> 00:16:35.575
it'll run perfectly.

00:16:35.575 --> 00:16:37.200
We have a lot of
experience doing this.

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:41.070
So Google was one of the
pioneers in container computing

00:16:41.070 --> 00:16:42.360
and Linux.

00:16:42.360 --> 00:16:44.405
And we've been doing
this for over 10 years.

00:16:44.405 --> 00:16:45.780
And one of the
important things--

00:16:45.780 --> 00:16:48.540
I'm not going to read
all the benefits of being

00:16:48.540 --> 00:16:49.980
able to compute this way--

00:16:49.980 --> 00:16:54.140
but as you can abstract a
lot of the dependencies out

00:16:54.140 --> 00:16:56.140
of your application, you
get all these benefits.

00:16:56.140 --> 00:17:01.290
Whether it's portability, speed,
the velocity of your team,

00:17:01.290 --> 00:17:02.700
security.

00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:06.750
The dev team knowing that what
gets shipped to production

00:17:06.750 --> 00:17:10.140
is going to run the
way they tested it.

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:12.250
And just, simple scalability.

00:17:12.250 --> 00:17:14.790
So one thing we like
to talk about at Google

00:17:14.790 --> 00:17:16.030
is sublinear scaling.

00:17:16.030 --> 00:17:16.980
So I don't know if
you've heard this term,

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:18.960
but it's the idea that
as your business grows

00:17:18.960 --> 00:17:20.640
and as your application
grows, your team

00:17:20.640 --> 00:17:23.490
doesn't need to scale in
proportion with the success

00:17:23.490 --> 00:17:24.780
of your business.

00:17:24.780 --> 00:17:25.740
Certainly, you wouldn't
want it the other way

00:17:25.740 --> 00:17:27.407
around, which is every
new user requires

00:17:27.407 --> 00:17:29.210
even more resources to support.

00:17:29.210 --> 00:17:30.960
So to give you a sense
of how this works--

00:17:30.960 --> 00:17:34.080
over the last 10 years, using
containers behind the scenes,

00:17:34.080 --> 00:17:36.360
we launch about 4
billion of them a week.

00:17:36.360 --> 00:17:39.960
Our ops fleet has grown 10
times faster than the ops team.

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:43.470
And that's really great order
of magnitude sublinear scaling

00:17:43.470 --> 00:17:46.065
that we can achieve
with this technology.

00:17:46.065 --> 00:17:47.940
So where does this appear
in managed compute?

00:17:47.940 --> 00:17:48.930
Well, the first
one I mentioned--

00:17:48.930 --> 00:17:50.700
on the left hand
of the spectrum--

00:17:50.700 --> 00:17:52.150
is Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:17:52.150 --> 00:17:54.150
So what this really is--
if you're familiar with

00:17:54.150 --> 00:17:55.440
Kubernetes--

00:17:55.440 --> 00:18:00.510
it's a way to manage a
container cluster on VMs.

00:18:00.510 --> 00:18:02.770
And you could either run
this-- it's open source.

00:18:02.770 --> 00:18:04.770
It's one of the most
popular GitHub repositories

00:18:04.770 --> 00:18:07.080
that has a lot of
activity and mindshare

00:18:07.080 --> 00:18:10.008
from the developer ecosystem.

00:18:10.008 --> 00:18:11.550
You can either run
it yourself or you

00:18:11.550 --> 00:18:14.280
can use GKE to let
GKE run it for you,

00:18:14.280 --> 00:18:16.710
manage the cluster
for you, update it--

00:18:16.710 --> 00:18:18.180
do all the security updates.

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:20.200
And then because open
source and portable--

00:18:20.200 --> 00:18:22.920
you can still interact with
the cluster master through

00:18:22.920 --> 00:18:26.010
kubecontrol-- or kubectl,
some people call it--

00:18:26.010 --> 00:18:26.680
directly.

00:18:26.680 --> 00:18:28.650
So the management can
be invisible to you

00:18:28.650 --> 00:18:31.025
because you're still interacting
with the cluster the way

00:18:31.025 --> 00:18:32.530
you're used to.

00:18:32.530 --> 00:18:34.775
If you'd like more management
of your applications

00:18:34.775 --> 00:18:36.150
and you really
don't want to deal

00:18:36.150 --> 00:18:38.837
with deploying applications
into a cluster,

00:18:38.837 --> 00:18:40.920
you might choose something
like Google App Engine.

00:18:40.920 --> 00:18:43.170
So I mentioned, this is in
the middle of the spectrum.

00:18:43.170 --> 00:18:46.020
Here, you can focus on writing
code in common languages

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:49.260
that might represent the
big App Engine Front End App

00:18:49.260 --> 00:18:51.850
icon or the big App
Engine Back End app icon

00:18:51.850 --> 00:18:55.372
here, and call other services
as needed to do the job.

00:18:55.372 --> 00:18:57.330
But you're really just
focusing on the versions

00:18:57.330 --> 00:18:58.247
of those applications.

00:18:58.247 --> 00:19:01.667
And letting App Engine scale
that application for you.

00:19:01.667 --> 00:19:03.500
And then finally-- this
is really exciting--

00:19:03.500 --> 00:19:06.215
is just Cloud Functions, where
you can literally go in--

00:19:06.215 --> 00:19:07.590
there's some other
great sessions

00:19:07.590 --> 00:19:11.310
where you see how short the code
is-- but just go into the UI.

00:19:11.310 --> 00:19:12.880
Enter the code.

00:19:12.880 --> 00:19:15.390
It could be several lines
that responds to an event.

00:19:15.390 --> 00:19:18.873
This event could be a new blob
is put in your GCS bucket--

00:19:18.873 --> 00:19:20.040
I want to do something that.

00:19:20.040 --> 00:19:23.040
I'll send it somewhere, maybe
do a little bit of processing,

00:19:23.040 --> 00:19:23.705
zip it up.

00:19:23.705 --> 00:19:25.830
That could be a very simple,
short, and sweet Cloud

00:19:25.830 --> 00:19:28.413
Function, where you don't need
to worry about where that Cloud

00:19:28.413 --> 00:19:29.070
Function runs.

00:19:29.070 --> 00:19:30.150
How to provision it.

00:19:30.150 --> 00:19:32.192
How to set up the networking
and the environment.

00:19:32.192 --> 00:19:34.358
Just give us the function,
and we'll run it for you,

00:19:34.358 --> 00:19:35.520
and automatically scale it.

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:38.413
And only charge you for
the resources it used.

00:19:38.413 --> 00:19:40.080
And so that's the
important part-- which

00:19:40.080 --> 00:19:42.360
is as you move
through the spectrum,

00:19:42.360 --> 00:19:45.450
we're very keen on making sure
you pay only for what you use.

00:19:45.450 --> 00:19:47.730
And, of course,
what you use varies

00:19:47.730 --> 00:19:50.520
based on how managed you want
that infrastructure to be.

00:19:50.520 --> 00:19:53.640
So in the Kubernetes world,
you're paying for the cluster--

00:19:53.640 --> 00:19:57.480
those are the VMs that
the cluster is running on.

00:19:57.480 --> 00:19:59.700
And the App Engine world
is paying for the instances

00:19:59.700 --> 00:20:01.283
that App Engine is
spinning up for you

00:20:01.283 --> 00:20:04.355
that's running the back end
and front end applications.

00:20:04.355 --> 00:20:06.810
And then Cloud Functions--
it's really just the management

00:20:06.810 --> 00:20:08.700
fee, the invocations
of the function,

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:11.640
and the actual compute cycles
run by the Cloud Function.

00:20:14.790 --> 00:20:17.118
So on this slide,
what I want to capture

00:20:17.118 --> 00:20:18.660
is just what I've
talked about, which

00:20:18.660 --> 00:20:20.610
is these different
levels of management

00:20:20.610 --> 00:20:21.735
that you can choose from.

00:20:21.735 --> 00:20:23.610
And so if you are choosing
Kubernetes engine,

00:20:23.610 --> 00:20:26.712
you're still saying you really
value the control over some

00:20:26.712 --> 00:20:29.170
of the cluster management, over
the application deployment,

00:20:29.170 --> 00:20:30.210
over setting that up.

00:20:30.210 --> 00:20:32.170
But if you don't
value that controller,

00:20:32.170 --> 00:20:33.600
that's just not
worth it to you--

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:35.850
you can go through the
spectrum and get to something,

00:20:35.850 --> 00:20:36.767
like, Cloud Functions.

00:20:36.767 --> 00:20:39.060
Where you're just focused
on writing the code,

00:20:39.060 --> 00:20:41.310
and pay only for what you use.

00:20:41.310 --> 00:20:43.047
And there's no right answer.

00:20:43.047 --> 00:20:44.880
You may be using multiple
these technologies

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:46.980
within your organization
because some teams value

00:20:46.980 --> 00:20:50.280
one and other team's
value the other.

00:20:50.280 --> 00:20:51.870
So I'm going to hand
it back to Debo.

00:20:51.870 --> 00:20:53.040
We'll talk to you
about cloud storage.

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:55.665
And then I'll come back to talk
to you about managed serverless

00:20:55.665 --> 00:20:56.560
Python.

00:20:56.560 --> 00:20:58.560
DEBO ADERIBIGBE: Thank
you, very, much, Stephen.

00:20:58.560 --> 00:21:00.480
Appreciate that.

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:03.270
So storage-- we've talked
a little bit about compute.

00:21:03.270 --> 00:21:06.192
And storage is the very
obvious analog for resources

00:21:06.192 --> 00:21:07.650
that you want to
make sure you keep

00:21:07.650 --> 00:21:11.820
track of when it comes to
your business and your value.

00:21:11.820 --> 00:21:14.760
Now, we talked a little bit
before about all the costs

00:21:14.760 --> 00:21:19.380
involved with running your
compute processes and workloads

00:21:19.380 --> 00:21:20.400
on-prem.

00:21:20.400 --> 00:21:22.980
And you have to think, very
similarly, when you're thinking

00:21:22.980 --> 00:21:24.675
about your storage workloads.

00:21:24.675 --> 00:21:26.550
I won't go through all
the different elements

00:21:26.550 --> 00:21:27.840
that we discussed before.

00:21:27.840 --> 00:21:30.180
But I, of course, like
to call your attention,

00:21:30.180 --> 00:21:32.610
very specifically, to the
idea that you have to think

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:35.250
about unavailability as a cost.

00:21:35.250 --> 00:21:37.470
When you think about
not being able to serve,

00:21:37.470 --> 00:21:41.550
either internally or externally,
the different types of data

00:21:41.550 --> 00:21:43.710
that you have to
process for people--

00:21:43.710 --> 00:21:45.557
that has a cost
to your business.

00:21:45.557 --> 00:21:47.890
And you want to make that
you're aware of that, as well.

00:21:47.890 --> 00:21:50.070
And you have to think about
repairability and other

00:21:50.070 --> 00:21:53.160
elements like that
on-premises, as well.

00:21:53.160 --> 00:21:55.890
Moving forward-- you also
have to think about the value

00:21:55.890 --> 00:21:58.140
questions that you drive
when you're making decisions

00:21:58.140 --> 00:22:01.140
with regards to storage on GCP.

00:22:01.140 --> 00:22:05.940
For example, can you handle
multiple or single disc

00:22:05.940 --> 00:22:07.320
failures?

00:22:07.320 --> 00:22:10.350
How do you optimize for
different types of storage use

00:22:10.350 --> 00:22:11.830
cases?

00:22:11.830 --> 00:22:14.640
How do you instantly access
the different types of storage

00:22:14.640 --> 00:22:17.130
that may hold the data
that's very important to you?

00:22:17.130 --> 00:22:19.990
And let's say, that you
want to go more granular.

00:22:19.990 --> 00:22:22.560
Let's say, I want to focus on
data servers, and structures,

00:22:22.560 --> 00:22:25.890
and buckets, or
rows, and databases,

00:22:25.890 --> 00:22:28.440
or in NoSQL
databases, and so on.

00:22:28.440 --> 00:22:30.000
How can I take
advantage of all that

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:32.870
from a storage perspective?

00:22:32.870 --> 00:22:36.310
So that brings us, of course,
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:22:36.310 --> 00:22:39.020
In a nutshell, it's
very simple and elegant.

00:22:39.020 --> 00:22:41.840
Just focus on creating
your storage buckets,

00:22:41.840 --> 00:22:43.940
put the data that
you need into them.

00:22:43.940 --> 00:22:46.790
And, of course, make sure that
you control your access, as

00:22:46.790 --> 00:22:48.950
necessary, from a
public, internal,

00:22:48.950 --> 00:22:50.390
or private perspective.

00:22:50.390 --> 00:22:53.420
And then once you're
done, remove that data

00:22:53.420 --> 00:22:56.242
and/or remove the bucket.

00:22:56.242 --> 00:22:57.950
Now, I want to take
a minute to make sure

00:22:57.950 --> 00:23:00.680
that you guys understand the
different classes of storage we

00:23:00.680 --> 00:23:01.340
offer.

00:23:01.340 --> 00:23:04.770
Because that's one of the great
values that we can provide.

00:23:04.770 --> 00:23:07.230
And one of the great ways you
can extract more value when

00:23:07.230 --> 00:23:09.230
thinking about your storage.

00:23:09.230 --> 00:23:11.870
When you think about
multi-regional storage--

00:23:11.870 --> 00:23:14.420
that's the most
frequently accessed data

00:23:14.420 --> 00:23:17.000
that you're going
to have in your set.

00:23:17.000 --> 00:23:19.520
More or less, it's going to
need to be access globally.

00:23:19.520 --> 00:23:23.100
And you want to have
low end user latency.

00:23:23.100 --> 00:23:26.540
And so, it's fantastic for
those global data sets.

00:23:26.540 --> 00:23:30.500
Then, of course, you might
move into more durable and more

00:23:30.500 --> 00:23:32.910
localized storage with regional.

00:23:32.910 --> 00:23:35.660
Let's say, you need to do
some analytics processing

00:23:35.660 --> 00:23:38.450
or you need to look
at some video encoding

00:23:38.450 --> 00:23:41.270
for a specific business
or internal purposes--

00:23:41.270 --> 00:23:44.570
that's more of your regional
data set or use case.

00:23:44.570 --> 00:23:47.780
Then we move into more of
the less frequently accessed

00:23:47.780 --> 00:23:51.920
storage or data use cases
with nearline and coldline.

00:23:51.920 --> 00:23:54.050
Nearline is for data
you typically want

00:23:54.050 --> 00:23:56.580
to access once a month or less.

00:23:56.580 --> 00:24:00.440
So we're thinking about
redundancy or backup use cases.

00:24:00.440 --> 00:24:04.993
And then, of course, coldline
is more of an archival use case.

00:24:04.993 --> 00:24:06.410
So you want to
make sure you think

00:24:06.410 --> 00:24:09.080
about your data storage and
some of those different types

00:24:09.080 --> 00:24:10.340
of classes.

00:24:10.340 --> 00:24:13.093
Now, I've told you-- just
as I did with compute--

00:24:13.093 --> 00:24:14.510
that I have different
ways for you

00:24:14.510 --> 00:24:16.490
to take advantage of
the different things

00:24:16.490 --> 00:24:19.010
we provide in Google
Cloud Storage.

00:24:19.010 --> 00:24:21.440
So once again,
I'll divide them up

00:24:21.440 --> 00:24:23.570
into different
types of behaviors

00:24:23.570 --> 00:24:26.300
and different types
of tools you can use.

00:24:26.300 --> 00:24:28.430
On the behavior
side, you definitely

00:24:28.430 --> 00:24:31.520
want to make sure that you
choose the right storage class.

00:24:31.520 --> 00:24:34.160
You want to make sure that if
you need to have high frequency

00:24:34.160 --> 00:24:36.560
data-- like, websites
or things like that,

00:24:36.560 --> 00:24:39.510
streaming media that you
need other people to access--

00:24:39.510 --> 00:24:43.460
you want to focus more on
the multi-regional section

00:24:43.460 --> 00:24:45.000
of the use cases.

00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:47.000
But if you've got
archival stuff,

00:24:47.000 --> 00:24:49.970
focus on putting it in
coldline as soon as you can.

00:24:49.970 --> 00:24:52.880
You want to make sure that
you properly tune your access.

00:24:52.880 --> 00:24:55.370
The last thing you
want, as a customer,

00:24:55.370 --> 00:24:57.650
is to think about
someone who you

00:24:57.650 --> 00:25:00.590
don't want accessing your data
going into your storage buckets

00:25:00.590 --> 00:25:01.580
and gaining access.

00:25:01.580 --> 00:25:03.080
So definitely make
sure you do that.

00:25:03.080 --> 00:25:05.120
Because that, of course,
increases the frequency

00:25:05.120 --> 00:25:06.800
with which you need
to serve that data,

00:25:06.800 --> 00:25:09.080
and might move it
across classes, as well.

00:25:09.080 --> 00:25:10.850
Then the last thing
I'd tell you to do

00:25:10.850 --> 00:25:13.970
is turn on Object
Lifecycle Management.

00:25:13.970 --> 00:25:16.910
Object Lifecycle Management
is particularly useful

00:25:16.910 --> 00:25:20.330
because you can move your
data across the storage

00:25:20.330 --> 00:25:24.110
classes I just introduced
to you, by creating policies

00:25:24.110 --> 00:25:25.890
that will do it automatically.

00:25:25.890 --> 00:25:27.150
So in some cases--

00:25:27.150 --> 00:25:31.130
if you're realizing that some of
your documents have a lifecycle

00:25:31.130 --> 00:25:32.990
and they're frequently
accesses first.

00:25:32.990 --> 00:25:35.120
But the less and less
relevant they get,

00:25:35.120 --> 00:25:36.830
the less and less
they get access--

00:25:36.830 --> 00:25:41.300
you can set specific policies to
move those things automatically

00:25:41.300 --> 00:25:43.910
from, say, multi-regional
or regional

00:25:43.910 --> 00:25:47.210
to coldline or nearline.

00:25:47.210 --> 00:25:49.100
On the tool side
of the house, you

00:25:49.100 --> 00:25:52.880
want to focus on using a single
API for all of those storage

00:25:52.880 --> 00:25:53.615
classes--

00:25:53.615 --> 00:25:54.740
that's particularly useful.

00:25:54.740 --> 00:25:57.500
Because you don't have to
change any of your code

00:25:57.500 --> 00:26:00.410
to access the storage
buckets that you're creating,

00:26:00.410 --> 00:26:02.720
depending on what
class they live in.

00:26:02.720 --> 00:26:06.410
Then we also have two things to
help you move your data around.

00:26:06.410 --> 00:26:08.980
We have a Cloud
Transfer Appliance

00:26:08.980 --> 00:26:11.300
and we have a Cloud
Transfer Service.

00:26:11.300 --> 00:26:14.390
The appliance is particularly
useful in helping

00:26:14.390 --> 00:26:19.190
you move your data
from on-prem into GCS.

00:26:19.190 --> 00:26:21.950
The Cloud Transfer
Service is for moving it

00:26:21.950 --> 00:26:24.230
from one cloud to another.

00:26:24.230 --> 00:26:26.130
So both of those are
particularly helpful.

00:26:26.130 --> 00:26:28.710
I encourage you to take full
advantage of both of those.

00:26:28.710 --> 00:26:31.050
And finally, there's
a storage growth plan

00:26:31.050 --> 00:26:33.950
to help you take care
of the volatility that

00:26:33.950 --> 00:26:35.950
comes from dealing
with storage costs.

00:26:35.950 --> 00:26:37.700
I'll give you a few
more comments on that,

00:26:37.700 --> 00:26:39.680
more specifically.

00:26:39.680 --> 00:26:41.690
The idea behind the
storage growth plan

00:26:41.690 --> 00:26:45.050
is to help you think about how
I'm going to deal with the fact

00:26:45.050 --> 00:26:47.960
that storage costs may
increase or decrease

00:26:47.960 --> 00:26:49.550
fairly unpredictably.

00:26:49.550 --> 00:26:51.590
So what we allow you
to do is if you're

00:26:51.590 --> 00:26:56.300
able to commit to $10,000
of spend over 12 months,

00:26:56.300 --> 00:26:59.510
then you can grow your
store data as you need to.

00:26:59.510 --> 00:27:01.550
That volatility
will just happen,

00:27:01.550 --> 00:27:05.300
and you don't have to worry
about overage during that time.

00:27:05.300 --> 00:27:07.520
At the end of that
commit period,

00:27:07.520 --> 00:27:10.190
you can either do one
of several options.

00:27:10.190 --> 00:27:13.730
The first saying, hey,
at my peak usage--

00:27:13.730 --> 00:27:15.800
I'm going to focus
on how much that

00:27:15.800 --> 00:27:18.680
costs, and deal with the
overages now and renew.

00:27:18.680 --> 00:27:21.020
Or I'm going to keep
my plan the same.

00:27:21.020 --> 00:27:23.630
Settle up on all the overtures
I incurred over the past year

00:27:23.630 --> 00:27:26.660
because I was able to think
about, and monitor, and predict

00:27:26.660 --> 00:27:28.070
over that period of time.

00:27:28.070 --> 00:27:31.770
And do this according to
growth storage plan terms.

00:27:31.770 --> 00:27:34.470
particularly useful when
you have a lot of storage

00:27:34.470 --> 00:27:36.340
and a lot of media
to take care of,

00:27:36.340 --> 00:27:40.850
but you have no idea at first,
what it's going to cost.

00:27:40.850 --> 00:27:44.440
So we've talked about the
value of Google Cloud Storage,

00:27:44.440 --> 00:27:46.900
let's talk, again,
about the total costs.

00:27:46.900 --> 00:27:48.460
One of the things
I'd imagine you'd

00:27:48.460 --> 00:27:50.230
really want to
take advantage of--

00:27:50.230 --> 00:27:52.030
especially if you're
just starting out

00:27:52.030 --> 00:27:53.660
with Google Cloud Storage--

00:27:53.660 --> 00:27:56.620
is to take advantage of
the always free tier.

00:27:56.620 --> 00:27:58.690
That is, we give
you a very fixed

00:27:58.690 --> 00:28:02.200
amount of regional storage,
a whole bunch of operations

00:28:02.200 --> 00:28:04.450
that you can use in terms
of moving your data around

00:28:04.450 --> 00:28:07.000
as necessary, and
network egress--

00:28:07.000 --> 00:28:08.238
that's always free.

00:28:08.238 --> 00:28:10.030
Just so you can get an
idea of how you want

00:28:10.030 --> 00:28:11.925
to move those workloads around.

00:28:11.925 --> 00:28:13.300
The other thing
to think about is

00:28:13.300 --> 00:28:16.790
that costs decrease by access.

00:28:16.790 --> 00:28:19.420
So you can look at the
prices per gigabyte month

00:28:19.420 --> 00:28:22.630
and see that multi-regional
storage might be 3/10

00:28:22.630 --> 00:28:24.380
of a cent per gigabyte month.

00:28:24.380 --> 00:28:27.790
But as you move down the
storage class spectrum,

00:28:27.790 --> 00:28:32.560
you'll find that coldline
storage is 4/100 of a cent.

00:28:32.560 --> 00:28:34.240
So if you optimize
your workloads

00:28:34.240 --> 00:28:37.373
for that kind of storage costs,
you can save a lot of money.

00:28:37.373 --> 00:28:39.040
Then, of course,
there's the third thing

00:28:39.040 --> 00:28:40.415
you need to think
about, which is

00:28:40.415 --> 00:28:42.970
the fact that you can pay as
you go or use a storage growth

00:28:42.970 --> 00:28:43.630
plan.

00:28:43.630 --> 00:28:45.100
We're giving you
two options based

00:28:45.100 --> 00:28:46.510
on how much you might know.

00:28:46.510 --> 00:28:49.840
And how predictable
your workloads are.

00:28:49.840 --> 00:28:52.300
Now, you guys might be
saying, Debo, this is great.

00:28:52.300 --> 00:28:54.430
This is super awesome we
got Google Cloud Storage.

00:28:54.430 --> 00:28:58.030
But I have to store things
at way more granular levels

00:28:58.030 --> 00:28:58.840
than that.

00:28:58.840 --> 00:29:00.470
What are my choices?

00:29:00.470 --> 00:29:04.195
I don't want to overwhelm you
guys, but we have quite a few.

00:29:04.195 --> 00:29:06.820
In fact, when you want to think
about all the different choices

00:29:06.820 --> 00:29:07.620
you have--

00:29:07.620 --> 00:29:10.210
there is very, very
ranging possibilities

00:29:10.210 --> 00:29:14.110
from thinking about
relational databases or NoSQL

00:29:14.110 --> 00:29:18.018
and in-memory databases,
block and file storage,

00:29:18.018 --> 00:29:19.810
and, of course, the
general purpose storage

00:29:19.810 --> 00:29:22.930
we were just talking about,
with Google Cloud Storage.

00:29:22.930 --> 00:29:24.880
The main thing I want
you to think about here

00:29:24.880 --> 00:29:27.820
is that it's also a spectrum--
very similar to what Stephen

00:29:27.820 --> 00:29:30.000
presented to you with compute.

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:33.700
In some cases, the main value
of using some of these services

00:29:33.700 --> 00:29:35.590
that we manage, some
of the boilerplate

00:29:35.590 --> 00:29:38.110
work that you have
to do for you,

00:29:38.110 --> 00:29:41.230
in exchange for an increased
cost, in some senses.

00:29:41.230 --> 00:29:45.250
Or a simple cost and a simple
management structure in others.

00:29:45.250 --> 00:29:47.470
And BigQuery is
included in there

00:29:47.470 --> 00:29:50.680
as one of our great best
in class analysis tools--

00:29:50.680 --> 00:29:53.230
that Stephen's going to talk
to you about just a moment.

00:29:53.230 --> 00:29:55.840
In any case, take advantage
of all the different tools

00:29:55.840 --> 00:29:58.240
and behaviors you can use
with Google Cloud Storage

00:29:58.240 --> 00:30:01.440
in order to get the most from
your dollar, in that sense.

00:30:01.440 --> 00:30:03.190
Now, I'm going to hand
it over to Stephen,

00:30:03.190 --> 00:30:05.710
to talk a little bit more
about the serverless platform

00:30:05.710 --> 00:30:09.111
and the analysis you
can do with BigQuery.

00:30:09.111 --> 00:30:10.361
STEPHEN ELLIOTT: Thanks, Debo.

00:30:10.361 --> 00:30:13.728
So the serverless
data platform--

00:30:13.728 --> 00:30:16.663
there's many products that can
be tied into a serverless data

00:30:16.663 --> 00:30:18.080
platform, but these
three give you

00:30:18.080 --> 00:30:19.747
a sense of what we're
trying to do here.

00:30:19.747 --> 00:30:22.710
And the echo should be loud and
clear from the managed compute

00:30:22.710 --> 00:30:24.550
section that we
just talked about.

00:30:24.550 --> 00:30:27.990
So BigQuery helps you
just write queries.

00:30:27.990 --> 00:30:29.850
Don't worry about
provisioning the database,

00:30:29.850 --> 00:30:31.200
the storage, the compute.

00:30:31.200 --> 00:30:33.720
Just analyze your data.

00:30:33.720 --> 00:30:36.090
Cloud Pub/Sub lets you
just send messages--

00:30:36.090 --> 00:30:37.710
send the data where
it needs to go.

00:30:37.710 --> 00:30:39.293
And then something
like Cloud Dataflow

00:30:39.293 --> 00:30:40.850
is a serverless
processing pipeline.

00:30:40.850 --> 00:30:43.080
So just process the data.

00:30:43.080 --> 00:30:45.300
Don't worry about a provision
that Hadoop cluster--

00:30:45.300 --> 00:30:47.760
for example.

00:30:47.760 --> 00:30:50.180
So BigQuery is a
product I really love.

00:30:50.180 --> 00:30:52.343
This is not just
me being biased.

00:30:52.343 --> 00:30:54.510
My wife is a user experience
researcher on BigQuery.

00:30:54.510 --> 00:30:58.538
So this is not just me
transitively loving BigQuery.

00:30:58.538 --> 00:30:59.830
But it's a really cool product.

00:30:59.830 --> 00:31:01.050
And what I love
about this diagram

00:31:01.050 --> 00:31:02.320
is it shares the scale of it.

00:31:02.320 --> 00:31:04.290
So it's based on Dremel--

00:31:04.290 --> 00:31:07.860
Google technology that we use
to power our internal analysis.

00:31:07.860 --> 00:31:11.550
And it's completely separated
the federated storage

00:31:11.550 --> 00:31:15.750
of the data that it's analyzing
and the analysis of that data.

00:31:15.750 --> 00:31:19.520
And what's especially cool here
is distributed memory shuffle

00:31:19.520 --> 00:31:20.020
tier.

00:31:20.020 --> 00:31:22.230
So basically, what
that is it's a way

00:31:22.230 --> 00:31:24.480
for compute to be
completely stateless

00:31:24.480 --> 00:31:28.727
and analyze a lot of federated
data, shuffling the memory.

00:31:28.727 --> 00:31:31.060
So that if any of the nodes
go down, there's no problem.

00:31:31.060 --> 00:31:32.940
The analysis doesn't fail.

00:31:32.940 --> 00:31:34.340
If any of the
storage goes down--

00:31:34.340 --> 00:31:35.017
it's no problem.

00:31:35.017 --> 00:31:35.850
It's highly durable.

00:31:35.850 --> 00:31:36.670
It's replicated.

00:31:36.670 --> 00:31:40.050
So we have exabytes scale
storage on the left.

00:31:40.050 --> 00:31:44.160
And we've got petabytes
within minutes scale analysis

00:31:44.160 --> 00:31:45.070
on the right.

00:31:45.070 --> 00:31:46.420
And if you just think
about that for a moment--

00:31:46.420 --> 00:31:47.430
I used to work at
another company

00:31:47.430 --> 00:31:48.622
before I came to cloud--

00:31:48.622 --> 00:31:49.830
and it would be Monday night.

00:31:49.830 --> 00:31:51.580
There was a Wednesday
metrics meeting that

00:31:51.580 --> 00:31:53.080
was very famous in the company.

00:31:53.080 --> 00:31:55.320
And I would hit contention
with every other product

00:31:55.320 --> 00:31:57.487
manager trying to do analysis
on our data warehouse.

00:31:57.487 --> 00:31:59.700
Because we didn't have
a million core cluster

00:31:59.700 --> 00:32:03.350
that could scale 1,000
compute cores for my analysis.

00:32:03.350 --> 00:32:06.100
I was always running against
other people on node-based data

00:32:06.100 --> 00:32:06.850
warehouse systems.

00:32:06.850 --> 00:32:10.280
So I really love this product.

00:32:10.280 --> 00:32:11.970
And what you can
use that with is you

00:32:11.970 --> 00:32:14.400
can use it as an
intermediate stop

00:32:14.400 --> 00:32:15.940
for where your data's going.

00:32:15.940 --> 00:32:18.390
So you might be using Cloud
Pub/Sub in your service data

00:32:18.390 --> 00:32:20.940
platform to send messages,
maybe from your internet

00:32:20.940 --> 00:32:23.160
of things endpoints.

00:32:23.160 --> 00:32:25.440
Stand up a Dataflow
pipeline so you

00:32:25.440 --> 00:32:28.417
can process all that
data going into BigQuery.

00:32:28.417 --> 00:32:30.750
And then, maybe, couple that
with Cloud Machine Learning

00:32:30.750 --> 00:32:32.670
so that you can
train on the data,

00:32:32.670 --> 00:32:36.570
develop models that are reliable
and give you the right insights

00:32:36.570 --> 00:32:37.380
from the data.

00:32:37.380 --> 00:32:39.810
And then run those very
same models in Cloud ML,

00:32:39.810 --> 00:32:43.530
to do prediction on
the streaming data.

00:32:43.530 --> 00:32:47.480
So again, what we really
want to focus on here

00:32:47.480 --> 00:32:50.908
is drive value from
only the pieces

00:32:50.908 --> 00:32:51.950
that you want to work on.

00:32:51.950 --> 00:32:53.867
So just write the queries,
just send the data,

00:32:53.867 --> 00:32:56.663
just create the
pipelines, just develop

00:32:56.663 --> 00:32:58.580
the models for machine
learning and then, only

00:32:58.580 --> 00:32:59.490
pay for what you use.

00:32:59.490 --> 00:33:01.340
So in the case a
BigQuery, what you use

00:33:01.340 --> 00:33:04.010
is there is a charge
for streaming ingestion.

00:33:04.010 --> 00:33:05.570
No charge for batch loading.

00:33:05.570 --> 00:33:08.420
And then there's a charge
for the storage of the data

00:33:08.420 --> 00:33:10.380
and for the analysis
of that data.

00:33:10.380 --> 00:33:12.380
And actually, the analysis
comes in two flavors.

00:33:12.380 --> 00:33:15.920
On-demand, which is just as
fast as what we see inside

00:33:15.920 --> 00:33:17.840
of Google, is something
that just charges you

00:33:17.840 --> 00:33:20.610
for any on-demand storage
based on data scanned.

00:33:20.610 --> 00:33:23.040
But if you have
large BigQuery needs,

00:33:23.040 --> 00:33:24.800
you can also opt for
a flat rate model

00:33:24.800 --> 00:33:28.670
where you ask us to reserve
BigQuery slots for you.

00:33:28.670 --> 00:33:30.440
And those are dedicated
slots that can

00:33:30.440 --> 00:33:32.390
be saved just for your jobs.

00:33:32.390 --> 00:33:35.340
And also, give you cost
predictability over time.

00:33:35.340 --> 00:33:37.130
So that's something to consider.

00:33:37.130 --> 00:33:39.860
In the case of Cloud Pub/Sub,
just pay for the data

00:33:39.860 --> 00:33:42.680
that you sent in Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:33:42.680 --> 00:33:46.610
Dataflow is the data process
plus the workers working

00:33:46.610 --> 00:33:47.152
on that data.

00:33:47.152 --> 00:33:48.818
And then, Cloud Machine
Learning is just

00:33:48.818 --> 00:33:51.470
paying for the resources
required to train your model

00:33:51.470 --> 00:33:55.930
and to apply it to data
to create predictions.

00:33:55.930 --> 00:33:57.900
So end-to-end, what's
really beautiful

00:33:57.900 --> 00:33:59.680
about a serverless
data platform--

00:33:59.680 --> 00:34:01.770
you just focus on
the through line.

00:34:01.770 --> 00:34:03.850
Where does the data come from?

00:34:03.850 --> 00:34:05.040
Where do you want it to go?

00:34:05.040 --> 00:34:08.440
And what transformations
do you want

00:34:08.440 --> 00:34:11.100
to apply to it on
its way to, say,

00:34:11.100 --> 00:34:12.690
your data analyst,
or your scientist,

00:34:12.690 --> 00:34:17.489
or your business manager in
the visualization dashboard?

00:34:17.489 --> 00:34:19.170
So we have plenty
of cases internally,

00:34:19.170 --> 00:34:21.630
where we actually do
very similar things.

00:34:21.630 --> 00:34:23.550
Comes into BigQuery
as a product manager.

00:34:23.550 --> 00:34:26.610
I do, actually, analysis of
my own products in BigQuery.

00:34:26.610 --> 00:34:28.830
And then power a Data
Studio dashboard with it

00:34:28.830 --> 00:34:31.110
so I can see what's going
on with my products.

00:34:31.110 --> 00:34:33.179
And it's all very, very
easy because I don't need

00:34:33.179 --> 00:34:34.871
to worry about the resources.

00:34:37.377 --> 00:34:39.460
So we've done a lot of
talking about total value--

00:34:39.460 --> 00:34:42.040
how to think about total value
of moving to a more cloud

00:34:42.040 --> 00:34:43.570
native way of doing things.

00:34:43.570 --> 00:34:46.812
And the costs that come
with deriving that value.

00:34:46.812 --> 00:34:48.520
So I want to spend a
little bit of time--

00:34:48.520 --> 00:34:50.620
and there's deeper
sessions later and next--

00:34:50.620 --> 00:34:52.620
but I want to spend a
little bit of time talking

00:34:52.620 --> 00:34:54.590
about cost management tools.

00:34:54.590 --> 00:34:57.100
So one thing we've
learned, just talking

00:34:57.100 --> 00:34:59.290
to many different customers
of different sizes

00:34:59.290 --> 00:35:02.180
is that regardless of
the size of the customer,

00:35:02.180 --> 00:35:04.670
there's some common needs
for cost management.

00:35:04.670 --> 00:35:06.250
And these are captured here.

00:35:06.250 --> 00:35:08.200
There's the need to see--

00:35:08.200 --> 00:35:10.450
to visualize what your
costs are in a way that's

00:35:10.450 --> 00:35:12.070
meaningful to you.

00:35:12.070 --> 00:35:14.020
And then to hold the
right people accountable.

00:35:14.020 --> 00:35:18.370
So if it's me or Debo that's
overspending on the BigQueries

00:35:18.370 --> 00:35:21.160
analyzing our products-- that's
something that we can analyze.

00:35:21.160 --> 00:35:23.410
And we can see where the
costs are going, if we need

00:35:23.410 --> 00:35:25.340
to do anything differently.

00:35:25.340 --> 00:35:27.548
Same thing for compute and
any of the other products.

00:35:27.548 --> 00:35:29.923
And then, of course-- if you
can hold people accountable,

00:35:29.923 --> 00:35:31.540
you might also want
to be able to just

00:35:31.540 --> 00:35:33.850
have control for peace
of mind and confidence

00:35:33.850 --> 00:35:36.462
that you can run your
workloads in the cloud

00:35:36.462 --> 00:35:37.420
with predictable costs.

00:35:37.420 --> 00:35:39.470
And with the right
level of control.

00:35:39.470 --> 00:35:42.203
And then, finally, you expect
to get some kind of insights

00:35:42.203 --> 00:35:43.870
or intelligent
recommendations about how

00:35:43.870 --> 00:35:45.370
to optimize your workloads.

00:35:45.370 --> 00:35:47.920
And again, this is true
regardless of what size

00:35:47.920 --> 00:35:49.330
of customer you are.

00:35:49.330 --> 00:35:53.320
And there's something
that you should do

00:35:53.320 --> 00:35:56.470
before you get to this phase.

00:35:56.470 --> 00:35:59.020
There's a whole talk on this
that a colleague, James Byers,

00:35:59.020 --> 00:35:59.770
will give on this.

00:35:59.770 --> 00:36:02.200
But the way we like to
think about cost management

00:36:02.200 --> 00:36:05.230
is we want to make sure that
you can manage your costs

00:36:05.230 --> 00:36:06.860
the way you organize your work.

00:36:06.860 --> 00:36:10.990
And this is, to a large extent,
is an offline discussion

00:36:10.990 --> 00:36:13.060
with your teammates, with
your colleagues about--

00:36:13.060 --> 00:36:14.500
how do we organize our work?

00:36:14.500 --> 00:36:17.265
How do we organize the value
we're delivering as a company?

00:36:17.265 --> 00:36:18.640
Do we organize it
by cost center?

00:36:18.640 --> 00:36:20.740
Do we organize it by apps?

00:36:20.740 --> 00:36:21.850
Front end versus back end?

00:36:21.850 --> 00:36:23.308
And again, there's
no right answer.

00:36:23.308 --> 00:36:26.410
And sometimes, a hybrid model
of organizing work and value

00:36:26.410 --> 00:36:27.190
is important.

00:36:27.190 --> 00:36:28.900
But once you have
a sense of how you

00:36:28.900 --> 00:36:30.820
like to track the
value of delivering,

00:36:30.820 --> 00:36:33.040
then we offer tools to help
you organize your cloud

00:36:33.040 --> 00:36:34.310
environment that way.

00:36:34.310 --> 00:36:35.540
So there's the organization.

00:36:35.540 --> 00:36:38.836
It could just be your
domain name-- mycompany.com.

00:36:38.836 --> 00:36:42.160
Folders that might represent
teams, applications,

00:36:42.160 --> 00:36:43.250
or cost centers--

00:36:43.250 --> 00:36:45.518
those are ways to
organize the projects.

00:36:45.518 --> 00:36:47.560
And if you're familiar
with Google Cloud Project,

00:36:47.560 --> 00:36:52.060
it's, kind of, the fundamental
unit of an environment.

00:36:52.060 --> 00:36:55.450
Where all resources
within a project--

00:36:55.450 --> 00:36:57.690
you can see the costs for
all resources in a project

00:36:57.690 --> 00:36:59.565
and the usage for all
resources in a project.

00:36:59.565 --> 00:37:03.010
And you can also control
access and policies

00:37:03.010 --> 00:37:04.120
via this hierarchy.

00:37:04.120 --> 00:37:05.620
And then, finally,
there are labels.

00:37:05.620 --> 00:37:07.745
So there may be things you
want to be able to track

00:37:07.745 --> 00:37:09.230
across your organization.

00:37:09.230 --> 00:37:11.110
For example, security
workloads, R&amp;D

00:37:11.110 --> 00:37:13.810
workloads, other
overhead workloads

00:37:13.810 --> 00:37:17.470
that might not belong to any
single branch in the hierarchy.

00:37:17.470 --> 00:37:20.433
And so labels are something
else to consider using.

00:37:20.433 --> 00:37:21.850
And the reason you
want to do this

00:37:21.850 --> 00:37:24.267
is to be able to answer some
of these evergreen questions.

00:37:24.267 --> 00:37:27.130
So talk about visibility,
accountability, control,

00:37:27.130 --> 00:37:28.700
and intelligence.

00:37:28.700 --> 00:37:33.070
The questions behind
those are fundamental,

00:37:33.070 --> 00:37:35.810
and they tend not
to vary that much.

00:37:35.810 --> 00:37:40.030
So what are the value and
costs of my applications?

00:37:40.030 --> 00:37:42.820
What direction are
those trending?

00:37:42.820 --> 00:37:45.820
Do I understand the
drivers of those trends?

00:37:45.820 --> 00:37:48.490
And then, finally, what
might I do to optimize?

00:37:48.490 --> 00:37:50.232
So optimization
might not simply be

00:37:50.232 --> 00:37:51.940
about reducing cost,
but just making sure

00:37:51.940 --> 00:37:54.490
that there's, again,
sublinear scaling.

00:37:54.490 --> 00:37:57.640
That growth and costs-- if
there's much higher growth

00:37:57.640 --> 00:37:59.112
in revenue, is absolutely fine.

00:37:59.112 --> 00:38:00.820
But growth in costs
that might be greater

00:38:00.820 --> 00:38:02.278
than growth in
revenue is something

00:38:02.278 --> 00:38:03.500
to keep an eye out for.

00:38:03.500 --> 00:38:05.625
And at any level of the
organization-- whether it's

00:38:05.625 --> 00:38:07.912
the CIO, the business
owner, the engineering

00:38:07.912 --> 00:38:09.370
manager, the
developer-- these tend

00:38:09.370 --> 00:38:12.208
to be the same questions we
always get support calls about,

00:38:12.208 --> 00:38:14.250
and we get into discussions
with customers about.

00:38:14.250 --> 00:38:17.560
It just different zoom-ins on
the fractal of cost management.

00:38:17.560 --> 00:38:20.680
And we provide tools to help
provide any level of visibility

00:38:20.680 --> 00:38:22.497
that you might need.

00:38:22.497 --> 00:38:24.080
So here's just a
summary of the tools.

00:38:24.080 --> 00:38:26.202
And there's, again,
another in-depth session

00:38:26.202 --> 00:38:28.410
on cost management that'll
go into these more deeply.

00:38:28.410 --> 00:38:31.673
But we provide
out-of-the-box cost reports--

00:38:31.673 --> 00:38:33.590
that you can slice and
dice your costs any way

00:38:33.590 --> 00:38:37.220
you want within the
Google Cloud console.

00:38:37.220 --> 00:38:39.810
If you want a more detailed
view of cost-- for example--

00:38:39.810 --> 00:38:41.570
you really want to
get into the labeling

00:38:41.570 --> 00:38:43.680
and you really want to
see things at an hour

00:38:43.680 --> 00:38:46.010
to two-hour granularity--
there's detail billing

00:38:46.010 --> 00:38:49.160
export, which sends all of
your cost data into BigQuery.

00:38:49.160 --> 00:38:52.450
Because that's the
best place we think

00:38:52.450 --> 00:38:54.500
we can send it for analysis.

00:38:54.500 --> 00:38:57.330
And then for control, there's
budgets, alerts, and quota.

00:38:57.330 --> 00:39:00.010
So one thing to keep in mind
there is budgets and alerts

00:39:00.010 --> 00:39:01.220
are a lagging indicator.

00:39:01.220 --> 00:39:04.070
What you spend is a little
bit of a lagging indicator

00:39:04.070 --> 00:39:05.630
for cost control.

00:39:05.630 --> 00:39:10.458
Quota, on the other hand, is
quotas set on usage by user,

00:39:10.458 --> 00:39:11.750
by certain sets of permissions.

00:39:11.750 --> 00:39:13.022
And that's proactive.

00:39:13.022 --> 00:39:14.480
That gets in front
of the spending.

00:39:14.480 --> 00:39:16.760
But depending on the
application, whether it's

00:39:16.760 --> 00:39:17.900
production, testing--

00:39:17.900 --> 00:39:20.600
you can imagine that
some usage of quota

00:39:20.600 --> 00:39:22.430
is more appropriate than others.

00:39:22.430 --> 00:39:23.990
And some usage of
budgets and alerts

00:39:23.990 --> 00:39:25.880
are more appropriate
than others.

00:39:25.880 --> 00:39:27.010
And the, finally, there
are recommendations.

00:39:27.010 --> 00:39:29.010
So different products
have their recommendations

00:39:29.010 --> 00:39:30.590
about how to optimize.

00:39:30.590 --> 00:39:32.300
One that Debo
talked about was VM

00:39:32.300 --> 00:39:34.142
right-sizing recommendations.

00:39:34.142 --> 00:39:35.600
And there are other
recommendations

00:39:35.600 --> 00:39:38.090
like that that we
can provide insight

00:39:38.090 --> 00:39:41.683
into how you use your committed
use discounts-- for example.

00:39:41.683 --> 00:39:43.100
And those just
help you figure out

00:39:43.100 --> 00:39:46.040
if there are opportunities
to optimize your spending.

00:39:46.040 --> 00:39:47.768
Or just reconfigure
the way you're

00:39:47.768 --> 00:39:49.810
doing things to get the
most out of Google Cloud.

00:39:53.170 --> 00:39:56.140
So to recap, I'll
bring you back to how

00:39:56.140 --> 00:39:57.520
Debo introduced the session.

00:39:57.520 --> 00:40:01.750
And we talked about
comparing value to cost.

00:40:01.750 --> 00:40:05.470
And in particular,
just trying to seed the

00:40:05.470 --> 00:40:07.435
thought that the usual
way of looking at this

00:40:07.435 --> 00:40:08.560
is a little bit inadequate.

00:40:08.560 --> 00:40:09.977
And then the
insidious thing about

00:40:09.977 --> 00:40:11.560
this is this the way everyone--

00:40:11.560 --> 00:40:13.150
I always think about things--

00:40:13.150 --> 00:40:15.640
is just, how does
the cost change?

00:40:15.640 --> 00:40:17.980
It's always harder to think
about how the value changes,

00:40:17.980 --> 00:40:20.500
or how the culture of your
team changes, or how the way

00:40:20.500 --> 00:40:22.510
you build things and
maintain the changes.

00:40:22.510 --> 00:40:26.680
But if we can move from this
model of thinking about costs

00:40:26.680 --> 00:40:30.250
and value to a model that
really thinks about what you get

00:40:30.250 --> 00:40:35.350
from moving to very flexible
compute platform, a highly

00:40:35.350 --> 00:40:37.960
diversified, and tailorable
storage platform,

00:40:37.960 --> 00:40:41.530
managed compute services,
a serverless data platform,

00:40:41.530 --> 00:40:44.305
and many other products that we
offer, and APIs that we offer--

00:40:44.305 --> 00:40:46.180
then what, I think, is
the most exciting part

00:40:46.180 --> 00:40:49.210
of this discussion is how
the value trend changes.

00:40:49.210 --> 00:40:53.380
And just, the gap that it
opens between what you're

00:40:53.380 --> 00:40:56.410
able to deliver to your own
customers and what costs you're

00:40:56.410 --> 00:41:00.910
able to reduce on the backend.

00:41:00.910 --> 00:41:04.610
So to tie it up, this is why
we call ourselves Google Cloud

00:41:04.610 --> 00:41:05.340
Platform.

00:41:05.340 --> 00:41:06.760
And we think the
more and more we

00:41:06.760 --> 00:41:09.700
can be the platform that
you're building value on,

00:41:09.700 --> 00:41:11.320
the more you can
focus on building

00:41:11.320 --> 00:41:12.580
what's next for your business.

00:41:12.580 --> 00:41:14.770
And that's why Debo and
I are at Google Cloud,

00:41:14.770 --> 00:41:15.910
it's a really exciting.

00:41:15.910 --> 00:41:18.193
People ask us, why are we
building product managers?

00:41:18.193 --> 00:41:19.610
And it's because
this is actually,

00:41:19.610 --> 00:41:21.493
a really exciting
part about our job.

00:41:21.493 --> 00:41:23.410
And we really look forward
to working with you

00:41:23.410 --> 00:41:25.620
as you do that.

00:41:25.620 --> 00:41:29.080
So before we break, I mentioned
some other deep dive sessions.

00:41:29.080 --> 00:41:30.810
A colleague of ours,
Manish Dalwadi,

00:41:30.810 --> 00:41:33.390
is giving an intermediate
talk on how to save money

00:41:33.390 --> 00:41:34.500
with Compute Engine.

00:41:34.500 --> 00:41:36.330
And how to get value
out of Compute Engine.

00:41:36.330 --> 00:41:38.520
I highly recommend that talk.

00:41:38.520 --> 00:41:40.680
We have another
colleague, Matt Leonard.

00:41:40.680 --> 00:41:43.860
He's doing a panel with
several industry leaders--

00:41:43.860 --> 00:41:45.960
one from Etsy, a specialist
leader from Deloitte,

00:41:45.960 --> 00:41:47.760
and a couple others--

00:41:47.760 --> 00:41:49.710
on cost control and
financial governance.

00:41:49.710 --> 00:41:51.085
And that'll be a
good one for you

00:41:51.085 --> 00:41:53.160
to get a sense of the
different methods of having

00:41:53.160 --> 00:41:55.050
good financial governance
and cost control,

00:41:55.050 --> 00:41:57.780
depending on your
organizational needs.

00:41:57.780 --> 00:41:59.850
And then on
Wednesday, I'm giving

00:41:59.850 --> 00:42:01.680
a talk with that Deloitte
specialist leader

00:42:01.680 --> 00:42:05.010
on actually using the
monitoring and cost control

00:42:05.010 --> 00:42:08.965
tools that we offer as
the cost management team.

00:42:08.965 --> 00:42:10.590
So we look forward
to seeing you there.

00:42:10.590 --> 00:42:12.810
And then, finally, another
session that we'd like

00:42:12.810 --> 00:42:16.200
to recommend is one run by
our colleague, Phill Coletti--

00:42:16.200 --> 00:42:18.702
that's Phill with two L's.

00:42:18.702 --> 00:42:20.910
And that's establishing
financial governance controls

00:42:20.910 --> 00:42:21.410
on GCP.

00:42:21.410 --> 00:42:23.410
And he's giving that with
[? Oralee Fontany-- ?]

00:42:23.410 --> 00:42:26.850
who's a product
manager on quota.

00:42:26.850 --> 00:42:30.120
And then, we'll be at the cost
management showcase all week.

00:42:30.120 --> 00:42:31.650
There's an ask
the experts booth,

00:42:31.650 --> 00:42:35.580
where you can schedule time
with one of the Google Cloud

00:42:35.580 --> 00:42:36.820
staffers who are here.

00:42:36.820 --> 00:42:39.390
And then, finally, if there's
one URL you want to write down

00:42:39.390 --> 00:42:42.312
down-- its
cloud.google.com/costmanagement.

00:42:42.312 --> 00:42:44.520
And we'll be posting all
the talks on cost management

00:42:44.520 --> 00:42:47.170
there and other resources there.

00:42:47.170 --> 00:42:49.740
So that's a great hub for
cost management resources.

